# Hybridization of MOFs and COFs: A New Strategy for Construction of MOF@COF Core-Shell Hybrid Materials

## abstract
The exploration of new porous hybrid materials is of great importance because of their unique properties and promising applications in separation of materials, catalysis, etc. Herein, for the first time, by integration of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) and covalent organic frameworks (COFs), a new type of MOF@COF core-shell hybrid material, i.e., NH2 -MIL-68@TPA-COF, with high crystallinity and hierarchical pore structure, is synthesized. As a proof-of-concept application, the obtained NH2 -MIL-68@TPA-COF hybrid material is used as an effective visible-light-driven photocatalyst for the degradation of rhodamine B. The synthetic strategy in this study opens up a new avenue for the construction of other MOF-COF hybrid materials, which could have various promising applications.

The exploration of new porous hybrid materials is of great importance because of their unique properties and promising applications in separation of materials, catalysis, etc. Herein, for the first time, by integration of metal– organic frameworks (MOFs) and covalent organic frameworks (COFs), a new type of MOF@COF core–shell hybrid material, i.e., $N H_{2}$ -MIL-68@TPA-COF, with high crystallinity and hierarchical pore structure, is synthesized. As a proof-of-concept application, the obtained $N H_{2}$ -MIL-68@TPA-COF hybrid material is used as an effective visible-light-driven photocatalyst for the degradation of rhodamine B. The synthetic strategy in this study opens up a new avenue for the construction of other MOF–COF hybrid materials, which could have various promising applications.
Porous crystalline materials, composed of both inorganic and organic materials, such as zeolites,[1a] metal–organic frameworks (MOFs),[1b–d] covalent organic frameworks (COFs),[1e,f] and porous organic cages,[1g–j] with well-defined topologies and inherent porosities have shown great promise for applications in catalysis, gas sorption and separation, etc.[1] Among them, MOFs composed of metal ions and organic linkers are a promising class of porous crystalline materials owning to their excellent properties including large surface area, tunable pore architectures, and diverse functionalities.[2] Similarly, COFs, one kind of rapidly expanding porous crystalline polymers entirely constructed from organic building blocks through reversible covalent bonds, are also receiving increasing research attention.[3]
Recently, MOF-based hybrid materials have been prepared by hybridizing MOFs with other functional materials, such as metal nanoparticles (NPs),[4] polymers,[5] and other MOFs,[6] which could possess the structural traits of individual components and exhibit new properties. For example, core–shell MOFs, prepared by encasing (encapsulating) one MOF within another, exhibit useful gas separation ability[6b] or greatly affect the gas adsorption behavior.[6c] Similar to MOFs, COF-based hybrid materials have also been developed.[7] In particular, the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@$ TpBD core–shell hybrid material displays a good photothermal conversion ability and shows great potential in phototherapy.[7a]
Here, different from the aforementioned hybrid materials, we report a strategy for controlled integration of MOFs and COFs to form well-defined and hierarchical hybrid materials, which remain challenging and have not been explored. As shown in Scheme 1, for the first time, we report the controlled growth of TPACOF with highly ordered pore channels and sheet-like morphology on the aminofunctionalized MOF $(\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{ML}{\cdot}68)$ to construct a new type of $\mathsf{M O F@C O F}$ core–shell hybrid materials, i.e., $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-68 $@$ TPA-COF, with high crystallinity and hierarchical porosity. As a proof-of-concept application, the obtained $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-68 $@$ TPA-COF is used as an effective visible-lightdriven photocatalyst for the degradation of rhodamine B (Rh B).
Scheme 1 illustrates the synthesis of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}68@\mathrm{TPA}-$ COF hybrid material. Briefly, after ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2^{-}}{\mathrm{MIL}}{-}68$ was prepared,[8] it was functionalized with a building unit of TPA-COF,[9] i.e., tris(4-formylphenyl)amine (TFPA), to form aldehyde-functionalized $\mathrm{NH}_{2^{-}}\mathrm{MIL}{-}68$ , i.e., $\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}68(\mathrm{CHO})$ (Step 1 in Scheme 1). The TPA-COF was then constructed on the surface of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-68(CHO) by covalently linking TFPA and tris(4- aminophenyl)amine (TAPA) through a convenient condensation reaction to form the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-68 $@$ TPA-COF hybrid material (Step 2 in Scheme 1).
As a typical MOF, ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2^{-}}{\mathrm{MIL}}{-}68$ , consisting of one-dimensional corner sharing $\mathrm{InO}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ infinite chains and the 2-aminoterephthalic acid ligands, was chosen in our experiment due to its thermal and chemical stability under harsh experimental conditions and its amino functional groups for further chemical reactions. Using the previously reported solvothermal method with slight modification,[8] $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-68 with rod-like morphology was synthesized (Figure 1a, and Figure S1a in the Supporting Information). After the surface functionalization of ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2^{-}}{\mathrm{MIL}}{\cdot}68$ with TFPA, the obtained $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-68(CHO) still maintained the similar morphology (Step 1 in Scheme 1, Figure S1b in the Supporting Information). Their crystalline structures were confirmed by the powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) (Figure S2, Supporting Information). The successful graft of TFPA in $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-68(CHO) was confirmed by Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectroscopy (Figure S3, Supporting Information), showing a typical carbonyl stretching band of TFPA at $1694~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ . In Step 2 of Scheme 1, by condensing
TFPA and TAPA in a mixture of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-68(CHO), and $o$ -dichlorobenzene/ethanol/acetic acid (20:5:1, by vol) at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ , the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-68 $@$ TPA-COF hybrid material was isolated as brown powder which is insoluble in common organic solvents (see the Experimental Section in the Supporting Information for details). As shown in Figure S3 (Supporting Information), the FTIR spectrum of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-68 $@$ TPA-COF reveals the obvious appearance of stretching bands originating from both $N_{ Ḋ }\mathrm{H}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{MIL}{-}68$ and TPA-COF, indicating the condensation reaction and the formation of hybrid material. Figure 1b shows a typical scanning electron microscopy (SEM) image of the obtained $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-68 $@$ TPA-COF hybrid materials, in which the sheet-like TPA-COF grew on the surface of ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2^{-}}{\mathrm{MIL}}{\cdot}68$ , and the thickness of TPA-COF shell is $50{-}200~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure S4, Supporting Information). The successful preparation of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ - MIL-68 $@$ TPA-COF hybrid material was further confirmed by the small and wide angle X-ray scattering (SAXS/WAXS) (Figure 1c), suggesting that the crystalline characters of the parental ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2^{-}}{\mathrm{MIL}}{\cdot}68$ were preserved in the hybrid material. Moreover, the presence of a new characteristic diffraction peak at $5.01^{\circ}$ (Figure 1c,d), originating from the (100) plane of TPA-COF, confirmed the formation of TPA-COF on the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}68@\mathrm{TPA}{\cdot}\mathrm{COF}$ . Note that the slightly broad diffraction peaks in $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-68 $@$ TPA-COF at $8.17^{\circ}$ and $9.41^{\circ}$ came from the overlap of the corresponding diffraction peaks of $N_{ Ḋ }\mathrm{H}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}68 Ḍ$ and TPA-COF (Figure 1d). Moreover, the thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) proved that the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-68@TPACOF hybrid materials has high thermal stability up to $500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure S5, Supporting Information).
The permanent porosity of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-68 $@$ TPA-COF hybrid materials was determined by the $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ sorption isotherms measured at 77 K (Figure 2a,b), exhibiting reversible type-I isotherms with hysteresis between adsorption and desorption curves. Note that there is a negative slope in the adsorption of ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}–\mathrm{MIL}–68$ at higher partial pressure, which may be attributed to the flexible skeletons of ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}–\mathrm{MIL}–68$ under high partial pressure.[8,10] The Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface areas of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-68 $@$ TPA-COF was calculated to be $539\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , which is higher than that of pure ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2^{-}}{\mathrm{MIL}}{-}68$ $(451\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ due to the presence of TPA-COF with surface area of $1136~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . Moreover, the pore size distribution of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-68 $@$ TPA-COF was estimated by the nonlocal density functional theory method, showing three major pores of around 10.9, 13.6, and 14.8 Å (Figure 2b) which are consistent with the reported values.[8,9] These results suggest the existence of hierarchical pores, further confirming the formation of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-68 $@$ TPA-COF hybrid materials.
Besides the structural characterization, the optical properties of the $N_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}G8@\mathrm{TPA}{\cdot}C\mathrm{C}$ F hybrid material were also investigated. As shown in Figure 2c,d, UV–vis diffuse reflectance spectroscopy (DRS) spectrum of $\mathrm{NH}_{2^{-}}\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}68@\mathrm{TPA}{\cdot}\mathrm{COF}$ displays a reflection edge at around $560\mathrm{nm}$ , corresponding to the band gap of $2.21~\mathrm{eV}$ (Figure 2d), which is smaller than those of pure $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-68 $(2.82~\mathrm{eV})$ due to the growth of TPA-COF with band gap of $2.32\ \mathrm{eV}$ on the surface as well as the effective expansion absorption of ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2^{-}}{\mathrm{MIL}}{-}68$ and TPA-COF. In addition, different from the physical mixture of ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2^{-}}{\mathrm{MIL}}{-}68$ and TPA-COF with two typical reflection edges, only one reflection edge was observed in $\mathrm{NH}_{2^{-}}\mathrm{MIL}{-}68@\mathrm{TPA}{-}\mathrm{COF}$ , indicating the formation of a new hybrid material.
All the aforementioned results demonstrate the successful construction of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ - $\mathrm{MIL-}68@\mathrm{TP}\pounds$ -COF hybrid materials. In our study, we have noticed that the aforementioned hybrid materials cannot be directly synthesized using ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2^{-}}{\mathrm{MIL}}{\cdot}68$ as the seed (Figure S6, Supporting Information), indicating that the surface functionalization of ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2^{-}}{\mathrm{MIL}}{\cdot}68$ is a prerequisite for the formation of $\mathsf{M O F@C O F}$ hybrid materials. Moreover, our synthetic strategy can also be used to synthesize other $\mathsf{M O F@C O F}$ hybrid materials. For example, when the amino-functionalized MIL-69, synthesized through the surface modification,[11] was used as MOF seed, the MIL- $69@$ TPA-COF hybrid material was also successfully synthesized (see the Experimental Section in the Supporting Information for details). Its structure was well characterized by SEM, TEM, PXRD, FTIR, and TGA (Figure S7–S11, Supporting Information).
As known, ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2^{-}}{\mathrm{MIL}}{-}68$ is an efficient visible-light-driven photocatalyst for the degradation of environmental pollutants.[12] As a proof-of-concept application, the obtained $\mathrm{NH}_{2^{-}}\mathrm{MIL}{-}68@{\widehat{\varrho}}$ TPA-COF hybrid material was used as a photocatalyst for the degradation of Rh B under visible light irradiation $(\lambda>420\mathrm{nm})$ 1 (see the Experimental Section in the Supporting Information for details). As shown in Figure 3a, the intensity of the typical absorption peak of Rh B at around $554~\mathrm{nm}$ decreases with time in the presence of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-68@TPA-COF. Compared to $\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ MIL-68, the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-68 $@$ TPA-COF exhibited better photocatalytic activity with the rate constant of $0.077\ \mathrm{min^{-1}}$ , which was about 1.4 times that of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-68 (Figure 3b). The enhanced photocatalytic activity of $\mathrm{NH}_{2^{-}}\mathrm{MIL}{-}68\textcircled{a}$ TPA-COF could be attributed to its increased BET surface area (Figure 2a) together with the smaller band gap (Figure 2d) compared to the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ MIL-68. As shown in Table S1 (Supporting Information), the photocatalytic activity of the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-68@TPA-COF hybrid material is comparable with or even better than other MOFbased photocatalysts for the photo-degradation of Rh B.
In summary, we have reported a new strategy for construction of porous crystalline hybrid materials through controlled hybridization of MOFs and COFs. The preparation and optical properties of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-68 $@$ TPA-COF core–shell hybrid materials with high crystallinity and hierarchical pore structure have been systematically studied. Importantly, $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-68 $@$ TPA-COF shows great potential as promising visible-light-driven photocatalyst for the degradation of organic pollutants. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first synthesis of MOF@COF core–shell hybrid materials. We believe that our strategy will open an avenue toward the construction of other MOF–COF hybrid materials, which might have various promising applications in the separation of materials, heterogeneous catalysis, electronic devices, etc.
